@@ -0,0 +1,49 @@
+---
+title: Explicit provider execution and heterogeneous boards
+kind: reference
+status: current
+owner: core
+last_verified: 2026-11-05
+related: [architecture, target_resolution]
+code_refs: [src/merlin/targetgen/providers.py, src/merlin/targetgen/provider_compose.py, src/merlin/runtime/boards.py, src/merlin/runtime/backends/device_simulator.py, src/merlin/kernels/matrix_shim.py]
+---
+
+# Explicit provider execution and heterogeneous boards
+
+Version2 support metadata may declare an exact base with `extends.target`,
+`extends.repo` and a full lowercase `extends.commit`. Reading metadata imports
+no provider code. `provider_compose.materialize(root, check=True)` verifies the
+selected `MERLIN_PROVIDER_BASE_PATH` and frozen composition inputs. It never
+fetches a base or silently regenerates an overlay. This check proves input
+identity; the target owns merge semantics and hardware facts.
+
+Board catalogs may declare a complete `per_hart` roster containing physical ID,
+core type, source ISA and known VLEN/DLEN/ELEN dimensions. Matrix eligibility is
+an explicit `matrix_hart_ids` list. Duplicate/out-of-range IDs and contradictory
+matrix flags refuse loading. Missing matrix facts permit no matrix image. A
+worker mask selects execution resources; it does not remove physical CPUs.
+
+`runtime.backends.device_simulator` invokes the selected matrix provider's
+`device_simulator(unit, config)` callback. A refused/missing provider never
+falls back to another simulator. Execution verifies the binary digest and exact
+image before/after running. Physical hart count and active worker IDs are
+separate arguments; per-hart ISA overrides remain present for inactive CPUs.
+The target owns launch arguments and memory regions. Availability and process
+success do not imply a timing model, RTL correspondence or certification.
+
+`kernels.matrix_shim` supplies a software ABI for signed-i8 contractions with
+i32 accumulation. Positive-stride inputs pack into K-major panels, the target
+kernel runs, and results scatter through the requested strides. Each caller
+owns an aligned workspace; there is no hidden shared scratch. Rank2 and rank3
+static signatures are supported. Allocation capacity, aliasing and effects
+remain caller obligations.
+
+The optional LLVM bridge uses the actual upstream flattened-memref function
+type and aggregate return. Emitting that bridge in LLVM IR avoids assuming that
+a C compiler's struct/sret convention matches upstream MLIR. The bridge checks
+static descriptor dimensions, allocates/frees one workspace per invocation and
+returns the supplied output descriptor. Native tests lower actual rank2/rank3
+MLIR through the selected compiler and compare every result independently.
+Those checks establish the exercised ABI cases, not arbitrary compiler,
+runtime or hardware qualification. Target OS context setup remains a separate
+target-owned obligation.
